      {
        "evidence_id": "evidence:planner-build-001:6",
        "source": "tavily:answer:a2e9a3c1-c742-4f2a-9124-2425dcbf73b1",
        "statement": "The production‑ready build plan for the Planner agent begins by defining a modular research structure that leverages the Strands Agents SDK to generate dynamic, prompt‑driven workflows and the Tavily API to provide secure, real‑time web search, high‑fidelity content extraction, and structured crawling, allowing the agent to ingest and synthesize external data on demand; an audit‑findings contract is codified as a canonical JSON schema that records each audit item’s identifier, description, severity, source, validation status, and remediation actions, and this schema is embedded in a dependency‑graph validator that constructs a directed‑acyclic graph (DAG) of task and data dependencies to ensure that all prerequisite nodes are satisfied before execution, while the planner’s loop integration is refactored to map Loop‑generated tasks into a single centralized Microsoft Planner plan, preserving hierarchical relationships (main task‑subtask) and synchronizing status across Grid and Kanban views; the execution design includes a handoff layer that translates Loop task payloads into the Planner‑compatible schema, enforces DAG validation, triggers Strands‑driven reasoning steps, invokes Tavily for web enrichment, and records results back into the audit schema, with production safeguards such as rate‑limiting, retry policies, observability hooks, and automated CI/CD pipelines to validate schema changes, dependency integrity, and end‑to‑end functional correctness before each release.",
        "provenance": "tavily-search-answer:a2e9a3c1-c742-4f2a-9124-2425dcbf73b1"
      },
      {
        "evidence_id": "evidence:planner-build-001:7",
        "source": "https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/machine-learning/build-dynamic-web-research-agents-with-the-strands-agents-sdk-and-tavily",
        "statement": "Build dynamic web research agents with the Strands Agents SDK and Tavily | Artificial Intelligence\n## Tavily: Secure, modular web intelligence for AI agents\n\nTavily is an API-first web intelligence layer designed specifically for LLM agents, powering real-time search, high-fidelity content extraction, and structured web crawling. Built for developers building AI-based systems, Tavily is engineered for precision, speed, and modularity. It offers a seamless integration experience for agent frameworks like Strands Agents.Tavily’s API is an enterprise-grade infrastructure layer trusted by leading AI companies. It combines robust capabilities with production-grade operational guarantees, such as: [...] ## Research agent example\n\nMany research agent implementations require extensive development efforts and rely on deterministic logic or workflows with constrained inputs and outputs. Alternatively, Strands enables developers to build highly dynamic agents through natural language. Strands agents use prompt engineering to dynamically generate varied output types and accept diverse natural language inputs seamlessly.Combining Tavily with Strands unlocks a new class of agents purpose-built for deep, dynamic research. Unlike hardcoded research pipelines, this pairing helps developers accomplish the following: [...] ```",
        "provenance": "tavily-search:a2e9a3c1-c742-4f2a-9124-2425dcbf73b1:https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/machine-learning/build-dynamic-web-research-agents-with-the-strands-agents-sdk-and-tavily"
      },
      {
        "evidence_id": "evidence:planner-build-001:8",
        "source": "https://techcommunity.microsoft.com/discussions/planner/planner-integration-with-loop-tasks/4465839",
        "statement": "Planner Integration with Loop Tasks\nWhen using Loop, and we create a task and we connect it to Planner, it will create a new Plan and put the tasks under that. To me, this is dysfunctional, because we want all tasks to be under the main Plan in Planner. I like to use the GRID view of the Plan, which enables me to make subtasks under the main tasks, but others prefer to use the kanban view. However you can't see the subtasks that are under the \"main task\" like in Grid view.\n\nThere's lots of videos showing how these parts of Teams work together, but in reality, it doesn't really work that well when it scatters tasks created in different parts to different Planners.\n\nAny suggestions would be helpful, and thank you in advance for your help.\n\nJim\n\nloop\n\nplanner",
        "provenance": "tavily-search:a2e9a3c1-c742-4f2a-9124-2425dcbf73b1:https://techcommunity.microsoft.com/discussions/planner/planner-integration-with-loop-tasks/4465839"
      },
      {
        "evidence_id": "evidence:planner-build-001:9",
        "source": "https://dev.to/aws/building-production-ready-ai-agents-with-strands-agents-and-amazon-bedrock-agentcore-3dg0",
        "statement": "Building Production-Ready AI Agents with Strands Agents and Amazon Bedrock AgentCore - DEV Community\n``` [...] ``` [...] ```",
        "provenance": "tavily-search:a2e9a3c1-c742-4f2a-9124-2425dcbf73b1:https://dev.to/aws/building-production-ready-ai-agents-with-strands-agents-and-amazon-bedrock-agentcore-3dg0"
      },
      {
        "evidence_id": "evidence:planner-build-001:10",
        "source": "https://aws-news.com/article/2025-07-31-build-dynamic-web-research-agents-with-the-strands-agents-sdk-and-tavily",
        "statement": "Build dynamic web research agents with the Strands Agents ...\nThis article introduces a powerful collaboration between Strands Agents SDK and Tavily, enabling developers to build dynamic web research",
        "provenance": "tavily-search:a2e9a3c1-c742-4f2a-9124-2425dcbf73b1:https://aws-news.com/article/2025-07-31-build-dynamic-web-research-agents-with-the-strands-agents-sdk-and-tavily"
      },
      {
        "evidence_id": "evidence:planner-build-001:11",
        "source": "https://www.snowflake.com/en/developers/guides/build-a-due-diligence-and-investment-research-agent-in-snowflake-using-tavily",
        "statement": "Build a Due Diligence and Investment Research Agent in Snowflake Using Tavily\nSubscribe to our monthly newsletter\n\nStay up to date on Snowflake’s latest products, expert insights and resources—right in your inbox!\n\nProduct\n\nSupport\n\nIndustries\n\nCompany\n\nLearn\n\nSnowflake logo [...] The most production-ready approach is to integrate structured financial datasets directly from the Snowflake Marketplace, such as LSEG Financials. However, for this demo, we will use a smaller custom dataset (created using the Alpha Vantage API) to keep the environment lightweight, transparent, and easier to understand while focusing on agent orchestration and tool integration.\n\n#### Option 1: Use Snowflake Marketplace (Enterprise-Grade Dataset)\n\nFor a production or enterprise use case, the recommended approach is to subscribe to a structured financial dataset directly from the Snowflake Marketplace.\n\nExample dataset:\nLSEG Financials on Snowflake Marketplace\n\nLSEG\n\nSteps:\n\nThis approach provides:\n\n#### Option 2: Load Your Own Financial Tables [...] You will learn how to create a Financial Agent that analyzes ticker-level fundamentals stored in Snowflake and enriches them with timely insights from across the web. Using Tavily’s real-time search capabilities, the agent retrieves recent news, regulatory updates, litigation developments, executive changes, and emerging risk signals that may not yet be reflected in financial statements.\n\nBy combining trusted financial reference data in Snowflake with Tavily’s up-to-date web intelligence, the agent performs contextual risk and opportunity analysis that goes beyond static datasets. This approach helps reduce blind spots between earnings cycles and surfaces early warning signals for buy-side, private equity, and corporate development workflows.",
        "provenance": "tavily-search:a2e9a3c1-c742-4f2a-9124-2425dcbf73b1:https://www.snowflake.com/en/developers/guides/build-a-due-diligence-and-investment-research-agent-in-snowflake-using-tavily"
      },
      {
        "evidence_id": "evidence:planner-build-001:12",
        "source": "tavily-extract:https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/machine-learning/build-dynamic-web-research-agents-with-the-strands-agents-sdk-and-tavily",
        "statement": "## Tavily: Secure, modular web intelligence for AI agents\n\nTavily is an API-first web intelligence layer designed specifically for LLM agents, powering real-time search, high-fidelity content extraction, and structured web crawling. Built for developers building AI-based systems, Tavily is engineered for precision, speed, and modularity. It offers a seamless integration experience for agent frameworks like Strands Agents.Tavily’s API is an enterprise-grade infrastructure layer trusted by leading AI companies. It combines robust capabilities with production-grade operational guarantees, such as: [...] ## Research agent example\n\nMany research agent implementations require extensive development efforts and rely on deterministic logic or workflows with constrained inputs and outputs. Alternatively, Strands enables developers to build highly dynamic agents through natural language. Strands agents use prompt engineering to dynamically generate varied output types and accept diverse natural language inputs seamlessly.Combining Tavily with Strands unlocks a new class of agents purpose-built for deep, dynamic research. Unlike hardcoded research pipelines, this pairing helps developers accomplish the following: [...] With Strands Agents and Tavily’s capabilities combined, the agents excel in gathering industry intelligence and providing organizations with real-time insights into trends, competitor activities, and emerging opportunities. Agents can conduct comprehensive competitive analysis, scouring vast amounts of online data to identify strengths, weaknesses, and strategic positioning of industry players. In the realm of technical research, these agents can rapidly assimilate and synthesize complex information from multiple sources, which can help accelerate innovation and problem-solving processes. Additionally, such agents prove invaluable for regulatory compliance monitoring by continuously scanning and interpreting evolving legal landscapes to make sure organizations stay ahead of regulatory",
        "provenance": "tavily-extract:25df1ed3-3d8d-404c-8d9f-7774c0a3dd6c:https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/machine-learning/build-dynamic-web-research-agents-with-the-strands-agents-sdk-and-tavily"
      },
      {
        "evidence_id": "evidence:planner-build-001:13",
        "source": "tavily-extract:https://techcommunity.microsoft.com/discussions/planner/planner-integration-with-loop-tasks/4465839",
        "statement": "## Forum Discussion\n\n[![jtpiazza's avatar](https://techcommunity.microsoft.com/t5/s/gxcuf89792/m_assets/avatars/default/avatar-8.svg?image-dimensions=50x50)](/users/jtpiazza/973449)\n\n[jtpiazza](/users/jtpiazza/973449)\n\nTin Contributor\n\nOct 30, 2025\n\n# Planner Integration with Loop Tasks\n\nNeed some guidance - We are starting to use Planner to keep track of a large number of tasks. Currently planner is setup with:\n\nApprox 10 buckets (in kanban view), and a list of tasks below each. We are using this mainly just for tasks, so the Timeline view isn't needed. We want to connect tasks recorded in Teams meetings, and in Loop to our main Planner. Whats the best work around for this?\n\nWhen using Loop, and we create a task and we connect it to Planner, it will create a new Plan and put the tasks under that. To me, this is dysfunctional, because we want all tasks to be under the main Plan in Planner. I like to use the GRID view of the Plan, which enables me to make subtasks under the main tasks, but others prefer to use the kanban view. However you can't see the subtasks that are under the \"main task\" like in Grid view.\n\nThere's lots of videos showing how these parts of Teams work together, but in reality, it doesn't really work that well when it scatters tasks created in different parts to different Planners.\n\nAny suggestions would be helpful, and thank you in advance for your help.\n\nJim\n\n[loop](/tag/loop?nodeId=board%3APlanner)\n\n[planner](/tag/planner?nodeId=board%3APlanner)",
        "provenance": "tavily-extract:25df1ed3-3d8d-404c-8d9f-7774c0a3dd6c:https://techcommunity.microsoft.com/discussions/planner/planner-integration-with-loop-tasks/4465839"
      },
